Let $a$ and $b$ be the semi-major and semi-minor axes of the ellipse, respectively, and let $c$ be the distance between the center of the ellipse and one of the foci. Then $c^2 = a^2 - b^2.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.5 cm);

pair F1, F2, C, O;

F1 = (-1,0);
F2 = (1,0);
C = (0,0);
O = (0,1);

draw(F1--F2);
draw(C--O);
draw(Circle(O,1));

label("$F_1$", F1, W);
label("$F_2$", F2, E);
label("$C$", C, SW);
label("$O$", O, N);
label("$k$", (C + O)/2, NW);
[/asy]

Since the circle passes through $F_1$ and $F_2,$ the distance from the center of the circle to $F_1$ is equal to the distance from the center of the circle to $F_2.$  In other words,
\[\frac{|C - F_1|}{\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}} = \frac{|C - F_2|}{\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}}.\]Then
\[|C - F_1| = |C - F_2|.\]Since $C$ is the center of the circle, $C$ lies on the ellipse, so
\[|C - F_1|^2 = |C - F_2|^2.\]This becomes
\[a^2 - 2b^2 + b^2 = a^2 - 2b^2 + b^2.\]Therefore, $2b^2 = 0,$ so $b = 0.$  Then $a^2 = 2b^2 = 0,$ so $a = 0.$

Let $c = 1.$  Then $k^2 = 1,$ so $k = \boxed{1}.$

