Solution:

response:
There are $\binom{9}{3} = 84$ ways to choose three points. There are $\binom{3}{3} = 1$ way to choose the point that is the same as all three of the other points. So the probability that three points chosen at random are collinear is $\dfrac{1}{84}$.

Alternatively, there are $\binom{9}{3} = 84$ ways to choose three points, and there are $\binom{3}{0} = 1$ way to choose three points that are collinear, so the probability that three points chosen at random are collinear is $\dfrac{1}{84}$.

[asy]
size(50);
for (int i=0; i<3; ++i) {
for (int j=0; j<3; ++j) {
dot((i,j));};}
draw((0,0)--(0,3)--(3,3)--(3,0)--cycle);
draw((0,0)--(3,3));
draw((0,3)--(3,3));
[/asy]

Therefore, the probability that three points chosen at random are collinear is $\boxed{\dfrac{1}{84}}$.

