If $B$ runs three times as fast as $A,$ then $B$'s speed is $\frac{1}{3}$ of $A$'s speed.  Let $x$ be the angle between $\overline{OP}$ and the $x$-axis.  Then $\angle AOB = 90^\circ - x,$ so $\angle AOP = x.$  Since $\angle AOB = 90^\circ,$ we have $\angle AOX = 90^\circ - x,$ so $\angle BAO = x.$

[asy]
unitsize(2 cm);

pair A, B, O, P;

A = (0.4,0);
B = (1.2,0);
O = (0,0);
P = (0,1);

draw((-0.5,0)--(2,0));
draw(O--P);
draw(P--A);
draw(P--B);

label("$A$", A, S);
label("$B$", B, S);
label("$O$", O, S);
label("$P$", P, N);

label("$x^\circ$", (0.7,0.6));
label("$90^\circ - x^\circ$", (1.1,0.6));
label("$x^\circ$", (-0.1,0.6));
label("$90^\circ - x^\circ$", (-0.7,0.6));
[/asy]

Since $\angle AOX = 90^\circ - x,$ $\angle AOB = \angle AOX + \angle XOB = 90^\circ - x + x = 90^\circ.$  Since $\angle AOB = 90^\circ,$ $\angle AOP = \angle AOB = 90^\circ.$

[asy]
