The direction vector of the first line is $\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -9 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix},$ and the direction vector of the second line is $\begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -18 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix}.$  Since the two lines are parallel, their direction vectors are scalar multiples of each other.  In other words, there exists a scalar $c$ such that
\[\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -9 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix} = c \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -18 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix}.\]Then $2 = 4c$ and $-9 = -18c,$ which means $c = 1/2.$  Therefore, the lines are parallel and their distance is 0.

[asy]
unitsize(0.5 cm);

pair A, B, C, D, E, F;

A = (3, -10, 1);
B = (2, -9, -2);
C = (-5, -3, 6);
D = (4/2, -18/2, -4/2);
E = (4/2, -18/2, -4/2);
F = (4/2, -18/2, -4/2);

draw(A--B);
draw(C--D);
draw(E--F);

label("$\begin{pmatrix} 3 \\ -10 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} + t \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -9 \\ -2 \end{pmatrix}$", A, N);
label("$\begin{pmatrix} -5 \\ -3 \\ 6 \end{pmatrix} + u \begin{pmatrix} 4 \\ -18 \\ -4 \end{pmatrix}$", C, S);
[/asy]

The distance between the lines is then
\[\sqrt{\left( \frac{2}{2} \right)^2 + \left( -9 \right)^2 + \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^2} = \boxed{\frac{15}{2}}.\]

