Since $\triangle ABC$ is isosceles, $BD=DC.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.4 cm);

pair A, B, C, D, M;

A = (0,11);
D = (0,1);
B = (-11/2,0);
C = (11/2,0);
M = (B + C)/2;

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(A--M);
draw(B--D--C);

label("$A$", A, N);
label("$B$", B, SW);
label("$C$", C, SE);
label("$D$", D, NW);
label("$M$", M, S);
[/asy]

Let $x = BD = DC.$  Then $AM = 11 - x.$

By the Angle Bisector Theorem, $\frac{BD}{DC} = \frac{AM}{BM}.$  Since $BD = DC,$ $BD/DC = 1.$  Then
\[\frac{11 - x}{x} = 1,\]so $x = 11.$

Then $BD = DC = 11.$  Since $\angle BDC = 3 \angle BAC,$ $\angle BDC = 3 \angle BAD.$  Hence, $\angle BAD = 120^\circ.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.4 cm);

pair A, B, C, D, M;

A = (0,11);
D = (0,1);
B = (-11/2,0);
C = (11/2,0);
M = (B + C)/2;

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(A--M);
draw(B--D--C);

label("$A$", A, N);
label("$B$", B, SW);
label("$C$", C, SE);
label("$D$", D, NW);
label("$M$", M, S);
[/asy]

Then $\angle AMC = 120^\circ.$  Since $\angle AMC = \angle BAC,$ $\angle BAC = 120^\circ.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.4 cm);

pair A, B, C, D, M;

A = (0,11);
D = (0,1);
B = (-11/2,0);
C = (11/2,0);
M = (B + C)/2;

draw(A--B--C--cycle);
draw(A--M);
draw(B--D--C);

label("$A$", A, N);
label("$B$", B, SW);
label("$C$", C, SE);
label("$D$", D, NW);
label("$M$", M, S);
[/asy]

Since $\angle BAC = 120^\circ,$ $\angle BAD = 30^\circ.$  Then by the Law of Sines,
\[\frac{11}{\sin 30^\circ} = \frac{BD}{\sin 120^\circ} = \frac{DC}{\sin 120^\circ}.\]Hence,
\[BD = DC = \frac{11 \sin 30^\circ}{\sin 120^\circ} = \frac{11 \cdot \frac{1}{2}}{\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}} = \frac{11 \sqrt{3}}{3}.\]Then $AM = 11 - \frac{11 \sqrt{3}}{3} = \frac{32 - 11 \sqrt{3}}{3}.$

Finally, the perimeter of $\triangle ABC$ is $AB + BC + AC = 11 + 11 + 32 = \boxed{54}.$

