The probability that a single die shows a number other than 1 is $\frac{5}{6}$. The probability that exactly two of the dice show a number other than 1 is $\frac{5}{6}\cdot\frac{5}{6}\cdot\frac{1}{6}=\frac{25}{216}$. Since there are $6^n$ ways to roll $n$ dice, the probability that exactly two of them show a number other than 1 is $\frac{25}{216}\cdot6^n$. We set this equal to $\frac{25}{216}$ and solve for $n$: \begin{align*}
\frac{25}{216}\cdot6^n&=\frac{25}{216}\\
6^n&=1\\
n&=\boxed{1}
\end{align*}

